effect-raymarch.summary:
I added the `raymarch` effect in `src/AnsiDemo/Effects/Raymarch/RaymarchEffect.cs`, declared as `[Effect("raymarch", PixelMode.Braille)]` and implementing `IPixelEffect`. It writes every pixel. The scene has a red sphere in the middle, a green and a blue sphere beside it, and a yellow sphere orbiting the red one at 1.2 rad/s. They stand on a checkered floor that has fog and fades to an even grey in the distance. One point light gives Lambert shading, Blinn-Phong highlights and soft shadows, the sky is dark, and the camera slowly circles the spheres and sways in distance and height.

The spheres are traced through a distance field with at most 40 steps and an early exit. Rays that miss a bounding sphere skip the march, and the floor plane is intersected exactly. Tracing runs on a coarse grid of at most 100├ù60 rays: 80├ù48 at 80├ù24 and 100├ù60 at 200├ù60 in Braille. The grid is allocated in Reset and traced in Reset and Update. Render only scales it up bilinearly and allocates nothing.

Perf in Debug at 200├ù60 Braille is about 4.1 ms per frame against the 33 ms budget.

`tests/AnsiDemo.Tests/Effects/Raymarch/RaymarchEffectTests.cs` has 13 tests:
- preferred mode;
- 4.1 determinism;
- 4.2 goldens t0, t1 and t4;
- 4.3 the t = 1 frame differs from t = 0 and LitCount ΓëÑ 39;
- 4.4 both modes at 80├ù24 and 200├ù60;
- resize gives the same frame as a fresh runner;
- 4.5 perf, with the Perf trait;
- N.1 banned-API scan.

I checked the goldens through text and colour dumps and they match the "Must look like" line. The three `.ansi` files are left in place, with no `*.actual.ansi`. The acceptance command passes: 0 warnings and 13 of 13 tests.

headless-host.summary:
I added src/AnsiDemo/Shell/HeadlessHost.cs with `public static int Run(AppOptions o, TextWriter stdout, TextWriter stderr)`.
- `--wav`: writes round(TotalSeconds┬╖44100) samples of Soundtrack.Text through WavFileSink, with SongPlayer looping off.
- `--frame` with `--scene`: renders that scene alone with SceneRunner.RenderAt, for 0 Γëñ t < Bars┬╖SecondsPerBar.
- `--frame` with `--effect`: renders SceneSpec.Solo in the effect's PreferredMode the same way.
- `--frame` alone: uses DemoRunner.RenderAt and turns an ArgumentOutOfRangeException into a usage error.
- Usage errors write the usage to stderr and return 2. They cover unknown scenes or effects, t out of range, NaN or infinite t, and options with neither Frame nor Wav.

Tests: I added HeadlessHostTestEffect ("test-headless", a time-dependent HalfBlock pixel effect) and HeadlessHostTests, with 19 cases. They cover:
- frames equal SceneRunner.RenderAt(...).ToAnsi() at 80x24 and at 123x41;
- the last valid step;
- the frame changing with time;
- out-of-range, negative, NaN and infinite t;
- demo t ΓëÑ TotalSeconds;
- unknown scene or effect, scene plus effect, size Γëñ 0, and neither Frame nor Wav;
- a `--wav` file in the temp directory: 44 + 2┬╖6 265 728 bytes, non-silent body, deleted afterwards.

The acceptance command passes: 0 warnings, 19/19 tests. The full suite passes too (645).

console-host.summary:
I added the live console host in `AnsiDemo.Shell`, one type per file under `src/AnsiDemo/Shell/`:
- **`ConsoleFrameWriter`** (public): `Append(StringBuilder, Frame)` writes each row with `ESC[row;1H`, sets colours only where Fg or Bg change (always at the start of a row) and ends with `ESC[0m`. It never writes a newline or clears the screen. `AppendTooSmall(sb, cols, rows)` puts the clipped `Window too small: need 80x24, have {w}x{h}` on row 0 and erases the rest with `ESC[K` and `ESC[2;1H ESC[J`. It skips `ESC[K` when the message fills the row so the last character isn't erased. It also has `TooSmallMessage`, `IsTooSmall` and `MinCols`/`MinRows`.
- **`ConsoleNative`** (internal): kernel32 `GetStdHandle`, `GetConsoleMode` and `SetConsoleMode` through `DllImport`.
- **`ConsoleSession`** (internal, IDisposable): saves and sets the stdout mode (adds VT processing), sets UTF-8 output without a BOM, enters the alternate screen and hides the cursor. If stdout is not a console it throws. If its constructor fails it undoes what it already did. `Write(StringBuilder)` encodes into reused buffers and does one write to stdout. `Dispose` restores colours, cursor, screen, encoding and mode, only once.
- **`ConsoleCommand`** (enum) and **`ConsoleKeyMap.CommandFor(ConsoleKeyInfo)`**: Q, Esc and Ctrl+C quit; Space pauses; Right and Left change scene.
- **`ConsolePlayback`** (public, so it can be tested without a terminal): `ForDemo(scenes, cols, rows, song?, loop)` wraps a `DemoRunner`. With loop it calls `Seek(0)` on the runner and the song when the demo finishes. `ForScene(scene, cols, rows)` wraps a `SceneRunner` and starts a fresh runner each time `Bars┬╖SecondsPerBar` has elapsed. It also has `Step`, `Render`, `Resize`, `TogglePause` (sets `song.Paused` too) and `SkipScenes(┬▒1)`, which seeks the runner and the song. The static `SceneStartFrom(scenes, t, offset)` clamps to the first and last scene.
- **`ConsoleHost.Run(AppOptions)`**: hooks `CancelKeyPress` as a fallback, opens the session and sets `TreatControlCAsInput` (only when input is not redirected). With `--scene` or `--effect` it plays that scene alone with no music. Otherwise it plays the demo and starts `WaveOutSink` with the soundtrack unless `--no-sound`; if `Start` throws, the demo plays silently and the error goes to stderr after the terminal is restored. Each frame it measures dt with a Stopwatch (clamped to 0.1 s) and reads the window size; while the window is under 80x24 only the quit keys work and only the message is drawn, but time keeps running. When the size changes it resizes the runner and makes a new Frame, then steps, renders, does one write and sleeps the rest of the 33 ms. `finally` stops the sound, unhooks Ctrl+C, restores `TreatControlCAsInput` and disposes the session; an exception then goes to stderr with exit code 1. It returns 0 when quit or when the demo finishes.

Tests are in `tests/AnsiDemo.Tests/Demo/`: `ConsoleFrameWriterTests`, `ConsoleKeyMapTests`, `ConsolePlaybackTests`, plus a `ConsoleTestEffect` registered as "test-console". They cover:
- the exact frame string, with no '\n' and no `ESC[2J`;
- the too-small message: its text, clipping, the full-width case and the one-row case;
- the key mapping;
- scene skipping on both a test script and `DemoScript`;
- pause, skip, loop and finish, checked on the demo and on a real `SongPlayer` (no audio device);
- resize keeping time, and a single scene restarting.

No test calls `ConsoleHost.Run`, creates a `ConsoleSession` or opens a device. The acceptance command passes: the build has 0 warnings and all 51 Console tests pass; the full suite (691 tests) also passes. I could not run the host by hand, because `Program.cs` is still a placeholder and this environment has no interactive terminal, so live playback, keys and terminal restore are untested and rely on review.

